WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
Did stretch cause these cells to divide, or did the experiment only make more cells

2
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:10.000
glow green? And how many experiments are hidden inside one dish?

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.680
Watch one bench before any results appear.

4
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:19.793
Place two competing models beside the dish.

5
00:00:21.373 --> 00:00:23.333
Model A says stretch is enough for division.

6
00:00:24.793 --> 00:00:29.293
Model B says stretch starts the cycle, but another signal is needed for completion.

7
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:32.193
Both predict more green cells.

8
00:00:33.453 --> 00:00:35.733
The green snapshot cannot choose between them.

9
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.980
A useful prediction sits where the models disagree.

10
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.360
A shared prediction cannot test the choice.

11
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.120
Here, the disagreement is completed division.

12
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:53.520
The first gold lock closes: write each model's numerical prediction before

13
00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.520
collecting an image.

14
00:00:55.583 --> 00:01:02.463
Now predict. Both paths are real observations, but only one separates these models.

15
00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:06.963
Is it more green cells now, or more tracked divisions later?

16
00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:10.443
Hold your answer while the two paths move.

17
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:18.817
Fucci is a two-color cell-cycle reporter.

18
00:01:20.077 --> 00:01:25.177
In its simplest picture, green marks entry into S phase, not completion of the

19
00:01:25.177 --> 00:01:29.837
cycle. The camera must follow one cell's identity across time.

20
00:01:31.217 --> 00:01:33.517
Follow that cell until it becomes two daughters.

21
00:01:34.877 --> 00:01:39.277
For division claims, track cells to division instead of counting green cells.

22
00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.110
Next, ask what received treatment independently.

23
00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.150
Every cell here shared one dish, one pipetting, and one stretch.

24
00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.490
If the dish is odd, every cell carries that oddity.

25
00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:58.210
When treatment is applied to a whole dish, the dish is the experimental unit.

26
00:01:59.233 --> 00:02:04.153
Measuring more cells describes that dish more precisely, but it does not repeat

27
00:02:04.153 --> 00:02:06.853
treatment. Fresh runs do.

28
00:02:08.093 --> 00:02:11.753
A new day with new dishes can expose run-to-run variation.

29
00:02:13.213 --> 00:02:17.793
Three separately treated dish pairs give three independent summaries per condition.

30
00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:21.347
A SuperPlot keeps both levels visible.

31
00:02:22.787 --> 00:02:26.147
Small dots show cells, while large symbols show run summaries.

32
00:02:27.567 --> 00:02:30.627
Run two now stands out instead of disappearing in the pool.

33
00:02:31.907 --> 00:02:36.267
A SuperPlot keeps cell variation visible while comparing independent runs.

34
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.980
The three locks now align.

35
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.440
In shared-dish cell experiments, lock the prediction, endpoint and independent unit

36
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.640
before the data arrive.

37
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.560
Break one link, and the conclusion changes meaning.

38
00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:56.340
With all three aligned, the claim, measurement and analysis answer the same

39
00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.560
question. Controls then test whether a dull explanation could produce that answer.

40
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.400
Return to the opening dish.

41
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.980
Six hundred glowing cells can be valuable observations.

42
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:15.380
They are still one treated unit, and green still marks S-phase entry.

43
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:19.100
More dots cannot repair a mismatched endpoint.

44
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.140
Trust the conclusion only when all three locks fit.
